1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,188
Tokenized stocks trade around the clock.

2
00:00:03,137 --> 00:00:06,171
The market that sets their price does not.

3
00:00:06,171 --> 00:00:12,864
In that gap, people quietly pay more for a share than it is worth, and
nothing in a normal swap stops them.

4
00:00:13,490 --> 00:00:17,736
This is every tokenized stock on Base, priced two ways.

5
00:00:17,686 --> 00:00:22,048
The reference price, from Chainlink, read straight from the chain.

6
00:00:22,048 --> 00:00:25,134
And what the market is actually charging.

7
00:00:25,134 --> 00:00:29,783
When those two drift apart, the difference comes out of the buyer's
pocket.

8
00:00:29,783 --> 00:00:32,516
Nothing warns you before you click.

9
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:36,371
SlipNope moves the check inside the trade.

10
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:41,007
As the swap goes through, the pool compares its own price to the
reference.

11
00:00:41,007 --> 00:00:43,820
Inside the band, it clears.

12
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:46,671
Near the edge, the fee climbs.

13
00:00:46,671 --> 00:00:51,438
Past it, the swap is refused, and the money never leaves your wallet.

14
00:00:52,050 --> 00:00:56,648
This is a real transaction on Base, sent by the pool itself.

15
00:00:56,597 --> 00:01:00,868
It tried to buy at a price more than six percent above the reference.

16
00:01:00,868 --> 00:01:04,645
The chain recorded a failure, and not a cent moved.

17
00:01:04,645 --> 00:01:07,391
That refusal is the product.

18
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,694
Three markets are live: Nvidia, Apple and Tesla stock tokens, each
matched to its own reference feed.

19
00:01:14,644 --> 00:01:19,631
Two of them were seeded exactly at the reference price, to the last
decimal.

20
00:01:19,631 --> 00:01:27,886
Fourteen swaps have already been guarded, from four different wallets,
and you can verify every pool and every refusal on the public chain.

21
00:01:28,489 --> 00:01:31,459
And a refusal is not a dead end.

22
00:01:31,409 --> 00:01:35,251
You can park the order at the most you are willing to pay.

23
00:01:35,251 --> 00:01:40,525
It waits there until the book comes back to you, and you can take it
back at any moment.

24
00:01:40,525 --> 00:01:43,284
The guard does not just say no.

25
00:01:43,284 --> 00:01:45,394
It holds your place.

26
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,390
The guard is open source, tested, and running on Base right now.

27
00:01:50,340 --> 00:01:54,519
Every pool, every refusal, every receipt is public.

28
00:01:54,519 --> 00:01:56,251
Overpay?

29
00:01:56,251 --> 00:01:57,748
Nope.

